  "meta": {
    "title": "Squeeze repeated letters (tr -s)",
    "blurb": "Collapses every run of the same letter to one copy: aaabba ↦ aba. The state is the last letter written, and a repeat of it writes nothing at all — an edge whose output is the empty string.",
    "inputs": [
      {
        "w": "aaabba",
        "out": "aba"
      },
      {
        "w": "abab",
        "out": "abab"
      },
      {
        "w": "bbbbb",
        "out": "b"
      },
      {
        "w": "aabbaabb",
        "out": "abab"
      },
      {
        "w": "",
        "out": ""
      }
    ],
    "library": {
      "author": {
        "login": "thethinkmachine"
      },
      "license": "CC-BY-4.0",
      "tags": [
        "text-processing",
        "unix",
        "epsilon-output"
      ],
      "difficulty": "intro",
      "readme": "The Unix command `tr -s` (\"squeeze\") as a finite-state transducer. Unlike a Mealy machine, whose every step writes exactly one symbol, an FST's edges may write any string — including none. The two self-loops write nothing, which is how runs disappear.\n\nWhat is left is the \"shape\" of the word: the alternating skeleton **abab…** with the repetitions removed. The transducer is deterministic, so every input has exactly one output."
    }
  }
}
